By the end of this exercise, I should be able to:

import polygon and tabular data with sf and readr;
derive rates instead of clustering on raw household counts;
use graphics and correlations to check the input variables;
compare hierarchical and spatially constrained clustering;
construct neighbours, edge costs, and a minimum spanning tree;
interpret cluster maps together with cluster profiles.

Analytical workflow
The analysis follows the Chapter 7 network workflow:



clean and project the event, boundary and road data;
match recorded events to the road network;
split roads into lixels and create line-centre samples;
estimate discontinuous network KDE and compare bandwidths;



use network K and G functions with a network-random reference;
use TNKDE to compare network intensity across time;
use cross-K and cross-G for fatal versus non-fatal events.


All distances are measured along the network. The main NKDE bandwidth is 1,000 m and the TNKDE time bandwidth is 30 days. The K/G and cross-K/G runs use 99 simulations over 0–3,000 m, so these envelopes are exploratory pointwise comparisons rather than formal global tests.

Public-safety implications
The results can support an initial screening process:

review road segments that remain visible across more than one NKDE bandwidth;
use TNKDE to match patrols or awareness work to the time periods where a corridor becomes stronger;
include fatality counts when ranking corridors, rather than using event counts alone;
check shortlisted locations against traffic volume, junction design, speed, lighting and emergency response time.

The maps do not estimate crash probability. They show where recorded events deserve closer transport-safety review.

This exercise follows Chapter 17. The aim is to compare three ways of measuring access to eldercare services in Singapore: Hansen accessibility, KD2SFCA, and the Spatial Accessibility Measure (SAM).
The workflow has four parts:

import and prepare the polygon, hexagon, service-point, and distance data;
calculate accessibility from a common demand-capacity setup;
map each accessibility surface and compare the distributions by planning region;
check whether the three methods highlight similar or different areas.







NoteReading the results



The demand and capacity values in this teaching exercise are set to 100, following the class example. The output therefore compares the behaviour of the accessibility methods rather than estimating observed service need. The local OD matrix is reconstructed from centroid-to-service distances because the supplied local data bundle did not include the original prepared OD_Matrix file.





By the end of this exercise, I should be able to:

import polygon and point data with sf;
check and update the CRS before using distances;
clean spatial attributes and derive demand and capacity fields;
tidy a long OD table into a distance matrix;
calculate Hansen, KD2SFCA, and SAM accessibility;
communicate accessibility patterns with maps, boxplots, and a method comparison.










TipMy observation



Accessibility is not just a map of service locations. It combines where demand is located, how much capacity is available, and how the distance between origins and destinations is weighted. The same service points can therefore produce different surfaces under different models.

